3.2.1 Multi-modal Encoders

Because property data and runtime measurements differ in sequence length and informational density, we use a modality-specific encoder E_m(·) to map each input modality x_m to a learned feature f_m ∈ R^{N_m}.

Tabular data encoder. RAN property data contain heterogeneous categorical and numerical features with complex dependencies across features and samples (Appendix A.3). We therefore build on SAINT [33], which tokenizes categorical and numerical features separately and assigns column-specific indices, preserving the distinct physical meaning of each RAN property. Its self-attention and intersample attention capture dependencies across features and across samples, respectively (Appendix A.1).

Time-series data encoder. Runtime measurements aggregate network behavior across spatial and temporal granularities, and adjacent timestamps are strongly autocorrelated. We build on PatchTST [27], which models interactions between fixed-length temporal patches and thus preserves this temporal locality, and extend it in two ways. First, within each patch, we add a channel-to-channel attention module to capture dependencies among measurement types. Second, to capture periodic and calendar-driven patterns, we replace standard positional embeddings with timestamp-aware embeddings: for each patch, we compute the average timestamp, extract calendar fields (month, day, and hour), embed them with trainable layers, and add the result to the patch representation (Appendix A.2).

