We use linear attention instead of softmax attention because the number of key and value tokens grows with the number of tabular features and time-series patches. Linear attention reduces the cost of cross-attention from O(L_T · L_K · d) to O((L_T + L_K) · d²), where L_K is the number of key and value tokens, and avoids storing the L_T × L_K attention matrix, which keeps pretraining on hundreds of millions of samples tractable. The output is the fused time-series embedding f_c.
